# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env (T-13): .gitignore and .dockerignore exclude it.
# docker compose reads .env itself; `make db-migrate seed dev-orchestrator dev-web test` source it into the shell.
# Variables that are commented out show their default; uncomment only what you change.

# ==== required ====
# shared secret web -> orchestrator (ADR-0003); generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# (the stack refuses to start while it is empty)
INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# ==== local development (README, "Локальная разработка") ====
# PostgreSQL password of the db container. The default "wom" is for a developer machine only: change it on any shared host
# and keep DATABASE_URL below in step with it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=wom
# path of the sandbox Docker daemon socket on the host (rootless daemon): /run/user/<id -u>/docker.sock
DOCKER_SOCKET_PATH=/run/user/1000/docker.sock
# for running migrations, seed and the dev servers on the host (the db container publishes 127.0.0.1:DB_HOST_PORT)
# DB_HOST_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://wom:wom@127.0.0.1:5433/wom?schema=public
# `make test` refuses to run against a database whose name has no "test" in it (the tests kill sessions): point it at a separate one, e.g.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://wom:wom@127.0.0.1:5433/wom_test?schema=public
# URL the BROWSER uses to reach the orchestrator WebSocket. The dev compose publishes the orchestrator on 127.0.0.1 only;
# for a browser on another machine set ORCHESTRATOR_BIND=0.0.0.0 and put the LAN address of the dev host here
# (e.g. http://<lan-address-of-dev-host>:3080). The production overlay derives it from WOM_DOMAIN.
ORCHESTRATOR_PUBLIC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3080
# ORCHESTRATOR_BIND=127.0.0.1
# "false" while the stack is served over plain http (local); "true" behind TLS (the production overlay forces true)
COOKIE_SECURE=false
# extra hostnames or addresses (comma separated) from which a browser on another machine may open `make dev-web`
# (the Next.js dev server blocks other origins); 127.0.0.1 is always allowed
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=
# Demo administrator for `make seed` on a dev machine (login demo). Without this variable `make seed` uses the password
# demo1234, and the Playwright e2e (E2E_USER / E2E_PASSWORD) logs in with it. The compose stack seeds NO demo account unless
# this is set; `make seed` refuses to run when .env describes a production host.
# SEED_DEMO_PASSWORD=
# `wom scenario push`: the platform to send scenarios to and the account on it (the CLI reads these from the shell, not from this
# file through compose; keep the password out of the repository and of chat logs)
# WOM_URL=https://game.example.com
# WOM_USER=
# WOM_PASSWORD=

# ==== production (docs/runbook-prod-install.md) ====
# The overlay publishes only Caddy (80/443); it needs the public host name of your instance.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml
# WOM_DOMAIN=game.example.com
# first administrator, created by ./scripts/deploy.sh (the password is never rotated from here after the first run)
# ADMIN_USERNAME=
# ADMIN_EMAIL=
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# false closes public sign-up
# REGISTRATION_ENABLED=true
# caps per client address (0 = no cap): active sessions across accounts, new accounts per day
# MAX_SESSIONS_PER_IP=3
# REGISTRATIONS_PER_IP_PER_DAY=5
# sessions on the host: all, and Kubernetes ones (the dev compose pins 10 and 3)
# MAX_SESSIONS_GLOBAL=20
# MAX_K8S_SESSIONS=6
# Kubernetes clusters created at the same time, and the time budget of their creation and of break.sh (ms)
# MAX_K8S_PROVISIONING=2
# PROVISIONING_TIMEOUT_MS=480000
# BREAK_TIMEOUT_MS=300000
# rootless Docker started with --detach-netns (Debian 13 packages): the namespace file egress-guard has to enter.
# Without it the guard cannot confirm its rules, stays unhealthy, and the orchestrator does not start.
# DOCKER_NETNS_FILE=/run/user/1000/dockerd-rootless/netns
# PostgreSQL data outside the sandbox data-root (bind mount). Set only AFTER the data has been copied there (runbook).
# deploy/hostwatch.sh measures this directory too (an absolute path, or /srv/pgdata when it exists).
# PGDATA_MOUNT=/srv/pgdata
# a PostgreSQL role of its own for the web app (no superuser, writes only the tables web writes); deploy/db-roles.sh creates it.
# Both or neither: without them web connects as the owner role "wom". The name: lower-case letters, digits, _; not "wom" or "postgres".
# The password: 16+ characters of letters, digits and . _ ~ - (it goes into a URL): openssl rand -hex 24
# WEB_DB_USER=wom_web
# WEB_DB_PASSWORD=
# feedback link shown in the footer, on the login/registration pages and on the session page: https://t.me/<chat> or mailto:<address>;
# empty = no links
# CONTACT_URL=
# a TCP proxy in front of this server (one public IP for several machines) that passes the client address with PROXY
# protocol: its address as a CIDR, e.g. 10.10.10.2/32. Empty = no proxy (Caddy ignores PROXY headers from everyone).
# WOM_TRUSTED_PROXY=

# ==== tuning (defaults shown) ====
# Prisma connection pool of the orchestrator and of the web app (each process): connections it may open, and how many seconds a
# query waits for a free one before failing. PostgreSQL allows 100 connections in all.
# DB_POOL_LIMIT=10
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT_SEC=20
# disk usage of DISK_GUARD_PATH (the data-root of the sandbox daemon, seen from the orchestrator container, percent; 0 switches a threshold off):
# refuse new sessions / kill every session
# DISK_GUARD_PATH=/
# DISK_REFUSE_PCT=85
# DISK_KILL_PCT=93
# a Kubernetes session is refused while the egress-guard heartbeat (EGRESS_HEARTBEAT_FILE, set by compose) is older than this
# EGRESS_HEARTBEAT_MAX_AGE_SEC=150
# build of a submitted scenario image (docker build of an author's Dockerfile)
# SUBMISSION_BUILD_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# limits of EVERY kind node (a session has up to 3-4 nodes): MAX_K8S_SESSIONS x nodes x memory must fit the host
# KIND_NODE_MEMORY_MB=4096
# KIND_NODE_CPUS=2
# KIND_NODE_PIDS=8192
# retention (days, 0 = keep for ever): session events, and the zip archives of finished submissions (replaced by an empty value)
# RETENTION_DAYS=90
# RETENTION_ARCHIVE_DAYS=180
# age public key: backup.sh then writes an encrypted copy of this .env next to every nightly dump (needs `age` on the host)
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=
# BACKUP_DIR=/srv/backups

# ==== orchestrator and web on the host (make dev-orchestrator / dev-web / test; docker-compose.yml does not forward these) ====
# /health of the orchestrator reports ok:false and the reasons in "degraded" when docker is unreachable, the reconciler has not run for this
# many intervals, or more than FAILED_ALERT_PER_HOUR sessions failed to provision in the last hour; deploy/healthcheck.sh turns that into an alert
# HEALTH_RECONCILER_STALE_INTERVALS=5
# FAILED_ALERT_PER_HOUR=5
# accounts without any activity for this many days are disabled (0 = never)
# INACTIVE_ACCOUNT_DAYS=7
# time zone of dates shown in the interface (read by the web app; default Europe/Moscow)
# WOM_TZ=Europe/Moscow
# debug / info / warn / error
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# ==== advanced (set by compose or autodetected; change only when you know why) ====
# public address used in robots.txt, sitemap and the page metadata; default https://${WOM_DOMAIN}
# SITE_URL=
# kind binary and the image of the cluster terminal (the orchestrator; empty KIND_BIN switches Kubernetes scenarios off)
# KIND_BIN=kind
# TOOLS_IMAGE=wom-tools:dev
# heartbeat file written by egress-guard (set by compose); a Kubernetes session is refused when it is stale
# EGRESS_HEARTBEAT_FILE=
# size of the cache of image archives of scenarios, MB
# WOM_IMAGE_CACHE_MAX_MB=10240
# the sandbox daemon: socket for the orchestrator on the host, and the CLI/make variable DOCKER_HOST
# DOCKER_SOCKET=/run/user/1000/docker.sock
# DOCKER_HOST=unix:///run/user/1000/docker.sock
# address of the orchestrator for the web app on the host (make dev-web sets it); NODE_ENV is set by the tools (production in the images)
# ORCHESTRATOR_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3080
# NODE_ENV=

# ==== Playwright e2e (make e2e; against a running stack) ====
# E2E_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
# E2E_USER=demo
# E2E_PASSWORD=demo1234
# E2E_KIND=1   # also runs the one Kubernetes scenario (takes a kind slot)

# ==== host monitoring (NOT read from this file) ====
# deploy/hostwatch.sh on the server reads ~/.wom-hostwatch.env (TG_BOT_TOKEN and TG_CHAT_ID are required; HOST_DISK_PCT,
# DISK_PATHS, LOAD_MAX, CERT_DAYS, BACKUP_DIR, BACKUP_MAX_AGE_MIN, KIND_NODES_MAX are optional).
# deploy/healthcheck.sh on the external machine reads ~/.wom-healthcheck.env: TG_BOT_TOKEN, TG_CHAT_ID (required),
# DISK_ALERT_PCT=85, BACKUP_MIRROR_DIR=<local copy of /srv/backups, warns when the newest dump is older than 26 h>.
